Contiguous Memory Allocation

Welcome to the first practical memory allocation technique in Operating Systems.

After learning:

RAM
MMU
Logical Address
Physical Address
Address Binding

Now the question is:

How does OS actually place processes inside RAM?

Answer:

Contiguous Memory Allocation
🧠 What Does Contiguous Mean?

Contiguous means:

Continuous
Adjacent
Without Gaps

Example:

Contiguous memory:

100
101
102
103
104
105

Non-Contiguous memory:

100
101
500
501
900
901
Definition

In Contiguous Memory Allocation, each process gets one continuous block of memory.

Example:

RAM

+----------------+
|      P1        |
+----------------+
|      P2        |
+----------------+
|      P3        |
+----------------+

Each process occupies one continuous region.

🏠 Real-Life Analogy

Imagine a train.

Contiguous allocation:

Seat 10
Seat 11
Seat 12
Seat 13

All together.

Non-contiguous allocation:

Seat 10
Seat 50
Seat 100
Seat 200

Scattered.

Why Was It Used?

Early computers had:

Very little RAM
Simple hardware

Contiguous allocation was:

Easy
Fast
Simple
Types of Contiguous Allocation

Two major types:

1. Fixed Partitioning
2. Variable Partitioning
🔹 Fixed Partitioning

RAM is divided beforehand into fixed-size partitions.

Example:

RAM = 1000 MB

Partitions:

250 MB
250 MB
250 MB
250 MB

Visualization:

+---------+
| Part 1  |
+---------+
| Part 2  |
+---------+
| Part 3  |
+---------+
| Part 4  |
+---------+
Example

Suppose:

P1 = 200 MB
P2 = 150 MB
P3 = 100 MB

Each gets one partition.

Looks good.

Problem

Suppose:

Partition = 250 MB

Process = 100 MB

Used:

100 MB

Unused:

150 MB

This unused memory cannot be used by others.

Internal Fragmentation
Definition

Unused space inside an allocated partition.

Example:

Partition = 250 MB
Process = 100 MB

Wasted:

150 MB

Visualization:

+------------------+
| Process 100 MB   |
|------------------|
| Waste 150 MB     |
+------------------+
Easy Memory Trick
Internal
=
Inside allocated block
Real-Life Example

You book:

4-seater table

but only:

2 people arrive

Empty seats are:

Internal Fragmentation
Advantages of Fixed Partitioning

✅ Very Simple

✅ Easy Implementation

✅ Fast Allocation

Disadvantages

❌ Internal Fragmentation

❌ Limited Number of Processes

❌ Poor Memory Utilization

🔹 Variable Partitioning

To solve waste, OS creates partitions dynamically.

Instead of:

250 MB fixed partition

OS allocates exactly what process needs.

Example:

P1 = 100 MB
P2 = 200 MB
P3 = 300 MB

RAM:

+----------+
| P1 100MB |
+----------+
| P2 200MB |
+----------+
| P3 300MB |
+----------+

Much less waste.

Problem Appears Again 😈

Suppose:

P2 finishes

Memory becomes:

+----------+
| P1       |
+----------+
| 200 MB   |
| FREE     |
+----------+
| P3       |
+----------+

Now another process arrives:

P4 = 250 MB

Question:

Can P4 fit?

No

Even if total free memory elsewhere is enough.

External Fragmentation
Definition

Free memory exists but is scattered into small holes.

Visualization:

+---------+
| P1      |
+---------+
| Free    |
+---------+
| P2      |
+---------+
| Free    |
+---------+
| P3      |
+---------+

Total free memory:

400 MB

Largest hole:

150 MB

Process needs:

300 MB

Cannot fit.

This is:

External Fragmentation
Easy Memory Trick
External
=
Outside processes
=
Free holes
Internal vs External Fragmentation
Internal	External
Waste inside allocated block	Waste outside as holes
Fixed partitioning	Variable partitioning
Memory allocated but unused	Memory free but unusable
