🧹 Compaction

Solution to External Fragmentation.

Idea:

Move processes together.

Before:

P1
FREE
P2
FREE
P3

After:

P1
P2
P3
FREE FREE

Visualization:

Before:

+----+
|P1  |
+----+
|Free|
+----+
|P2  |
+----+
|Free|
+----+
|P3  |
+----+

After:

+----+
|P1  |
+----+
|P2  |
+----+
|P3  |
+----+
|FREE|
|FREE|
+----+

Now one large block exists.

Problem with Compaction

Moving processes costs:

CPU Time
Memory Operations
Overhead

Therefore:

Compaction is expensive.

Memory Allocation Strategies

Suppose free holes:

100 MB
500 MB
200 MB
300 MB

Process needs:

180 MB

Where should OS place it?

Three famous algorithms answer this.

1️⃣ First Fit

Take the first hole large enough.

Example:

100 MB ❌

500 MB ✅

Allocate here.

Result:

Fastest
Advantage

✅ Very Fast

Disadvantage

❌ Creates fragmentation over time.

2️⃣ Best Fit

Choose the smallest hole that can fit.

Example:

100 MB ❌

500 MB

200 MB ✅

300 MB

Choose:

200 MB

because it wastes least memory.

Advantage

✅ Less immediate waste

Disadvantage

❌ Must search entire list

❌ Slower

3️⃣ Worst Fit

Choose the largest hole.

Example:

100 MB

500 MB ✅

200 MB

300 MB

Choose:

500 MB

Idea:

Leave larger leftover pieces.

Advantage

✅ Large leftover holes remain

Disadvantage

❌ Usually poor utilization

Comparison
Algorithm	Strategy
First Fit	First suitable hole
Best Fit	Smallest suitable hole
Worst Fit	Largest hole
Which One Is Used Most?

Generally:

First Fit

because:

Simple
Fast
Good Performance
